module

public import Sheaf.CategoryTheory.SheafDgModuleLocalization
import WeilConjectures.Topology.SheafCokernelLocalImage

/-! # A set of nonzero acyclic sheaf dg subobjects

Source: Stacks Project, sdga.tex, Tag 0FST, lemma-small-acyclics, including the
nonzero conclusion in its proof (blueprint: lem-sfb-sheaf-dg-small-acyclic-subobjects).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits TopologicalSpace

namespace CategoryTheory.SheafDg

/-- A set of nonzero acyclic left sheaf dg modules detects every nonzero acyclic
module by a monomorphism. Nonzero refers to a graded component, before homotopy
or derived localization. Cohomology is the underlying coefficient-sheaf cohomology.

Source: Stacks Project, sdga.tex, Tag 0FST, lemma-small-acyclics, final paragraph,
with left modules obtained by the graded opposite algebra
(blueprint: lem-sfb-sheaf-dg-small-acyclic-subobjects).

Proof:
1. Choose an infinite cardinal lambda bounding the opens and all graded algebra
sections; put mu = 2^lambda, so mu^lambda = mu. In a nonzero acyclic M choose a
nonzero section x. The generated-subsheaf size lemma
[lem-sfb-generated-sheaf-dg-submodule-size], applied to zero and x, gives F_0
containing x of size at most mu.
2. For every cycle section of F_r choose local primitives in M. Acyclicity and
[lem-weil-pbc-sheaf-cokernel-local-image] give these primitives. Choose at most
lambda opens per cover. The cycle triples and all chosen primitives number at
most mu. The generated-subsheaf lemma gives F_(r+1) containing F_r and the
primitives, still of size at most mu.
3. Generate F_infty by all sections of all F_r, again using the size lemma.
Its local formula says each section locally belongs to some F_r: only finitely
many generators occur locally. A cycle is therefore locally a cycle of F_r,
since F_r embeds sectionwise in M, and locally a boundary in F_(r+1). The
cokernel local-image criterion makes every cohomology sheaf zero. The original
x remains nonzero. This is the sheaf generated by the union, rather than an
assumption that a sectionwise union is a sheaf.
4. Fix a carrier set B of cardinal mu. Encode bounded objects by subsets of B
for each open and degree, and functions recording their group, scalar,
restriction, differential and action operations. Such codes form a set; retain
the sheaf dg identities, nonzeroness and acyclicity. Transfer each bounded
object along injections of its section sets into B. Thus F_infty is isomorphic
to some coded N_s. Compose that isomorphism with F_infty's inclusion in M.
The result is sectionwise injective, hence monic. If there are no nonzero
acyclic modules, the indexing set can be empty. -/
theorem exists_small_acyclic_subobjects (X : Type u) [TopologicalSpace X]
    (S : Sheaf (Opens.grothendieckTopology X) CommRingCat.{max u v})
    (A : Algebra S) :
    ∃ (I : Type (max u v)) (N : I → LeftModule A),
      (∀ i : I, (∃ n : ℤ, ¬ IsZero ((N i).X.X n)) ∧
        ∀ n : ℤ, IsZero ((cohomology A n).obj ((q A).obj (N i)))) ∧
      ∀ M : LeftModule A, (∃ n : ℤ, ¬ IsZero (M.X.X n)) →
        (∀ n : ℤ, IsZero ((cohomology A n).obj ((q A).obj M))) →
        ∃ (i : I) (f : N i ⟶ M), Mono f := by
  sorry

end CategoryTheory.SheafDg
